# 15. Branches & Variations

## OPTICS ⭐⭐⭐⭐

**Ordering Points To Identify the Clustering Structure**

Useful when clusters have varying densities.

It avoids relying on a single global `eps` in the same restrictive way as DBSCAN.

---

## HDBSCAN ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐

**Hierarchical Density-Based Spatial Clustering of Applications with Noise**

It extends density-based clustering using a hierarchy and is often useful for varying-density clusters.

Conceptually:

```text
DBSCAN
   ↓
Density hierarchy
   ↓
More flexible cluster structure
```

HDBSCAN is popular in modern workflows such as clustering embeddings.

---

# 16. Trade-Off Analysis

## Advantages

✅ No need to specify K  
✅ Detects noise/outliers  
✅ Handles complex shapes  
✅ No centroid initialization  
✅ Strong for spatial/density-based data  

---

## Disadvantages

❌ Sensitive to `eps`  
❌ Parameter tuning can be difficult  
❌ Struggles with strongly varying densities  
❌ Can struggle in high dimensions  
❌ Distance calculations become less meaningful in some feature spaces  

---

# 17. DBSCAN vs K-Means — When to Use Which?

## Use K-Means when:

✔ You expect a known number of groups  
✔ Clusters are reasonably compact  
✔ You want simple, scalable clustering  

## Use DBSCAN when:

✔ Number of clusters is unknown  
✔ You expect noise/outliers  
✔ Cluster shapes may be irregular  
✔ Density-based grouping makes sense  

